[[17592186152592 :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $6$?\n\n1. $R_{5}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $5$\n2. $R^{5}$\n3. $M_{6}(R)$, the vector space of all $6 \\cdot 6$ matrices over $R$"] [17592186152592 :question/answer-fields 17592186150029] [17592186069238 :question/problem "What is $lim_(x → 0)((x^{2} - 1)^{2} - 1)/(x^{2})$?"] [17592186069238 :question/answer-fields 17592186066576] [17592186060026 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\n1. $det(M) = 10$\n2. $det(M) =-5$\n3. $M$ is a singular matrix\n\n$M = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186060026 :question/answer-fields 17592186056484] [17592186229717 :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n$y^{″} + y = \\sec^{3} x$.\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n$y(x) = A\\cos x + B\\sin x + g(x)$,\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)$?\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result:*\n$\\cos 2x = 1 - 2\\sin^{2} x$"] [17592186229717 :question/answer-fields 17592186228445] [17592186175826 :question/problem "$\\mathbf{f}(t,z) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: 4\\sin{t}, \\: z \\big\\rangle$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\dfrac{x^2}{4}+\\dfrac{y^2}{16}=1$. What is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$?"] [17592186175826 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{4}+\\dfrac{y^2}{16}=1\n$$\n\nin $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\langle x,y \\rangle = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 4\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi)\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + \\frac{y^{2}}{16} &= ((2\\cos t)^{2})/(4) + ((4\\sin t)^{2})/(16) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4\\cos^{2} t}{4} + \\frac{16\\sin^{2} t}{16} \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} t + \\sin^{2} t \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(t, z) &= ⟨x, y, z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2\\cos t, 4\\sin t, z⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $z \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}2\\cos{t}}$."] [17592186175828 :question/problem "$\\mathbf{f}(x,y) = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: \\text{___} \\big\\rangle$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $2x^2=z$. What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$?"] [17592186175828 :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y) &= ⟨x, y, z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨x, y, 2x^{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ and $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue} 2x^2}$."] [17592186175829 :question/problem "$\\mathbf{f}(t,y) = \\big \\langle 4\\tan{t}, \\, y, \\, \\text{_____} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad t\\in\\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2,\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,y)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\dfrac{z^2}{4} - \\dfrac{x^2}{16} = 1$ for $z \\geq 2$. What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$?"] [17592186175829 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{z^2}{4} - \\dfrac{x^2}{16} = 1\n$$\n\nfor\n\n$$\nz \\geq 2\n$$\n\nin $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\langle x, z \\rangle = \\big \\langle 4\\tan{t}, \\, 2\\sec{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad t \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{z^{2}}{4} - \\frac{x^{2}}{16} &= ((2\\sec t)^{2})/(4) - ((4\\tan t)^{2})/(16) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4\\sec^{2} t}{4} - \\frac{16\\tan^{2} t}{16} \\\\\n&= \\sec^{2} t - \\tan^{2} t \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(t, y) &= ⟨x, y, z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨4\\tan t, y, 2\\sec t⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nt \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nand $y \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}2\\sec{t}}$."] [17592186176199 :question/problem "$f(x, z) = ⟨x, ___, z⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(x, z)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $y = 4x^{2}$. What is the second component of $f$?"] [17592186176199 :question/answer-fields 17592186173422] [17592186176201 :question/problem "$f(t, z) = ⟨3\\sec t, _____, z⟩, t ∈ (-\\frac{π}{2}, \\frac{π}{2})$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t, z)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x^{2} - y^{2} = 9$ for $x \\ge 3$. What is the second component of $f$?"] [17592186176201 :question/answer-fields 17592186173434] [17592186129861 :question/problem "Let $R = [- 5,-2] \\cdot [- 6,-1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}, P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the double integral\n\n$∬_(R)f(x, y)dA$,\n\nwhere $f$ is a function such that $m_{ij} = M_{ij} = 3$ for all partitions $P$ of $R$, and $m_{ij}$ and $M_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion $R_{ij}$."] [17592186129861 :question/answer-fields 17592186128617] [17592186161811 :question/problem "Given that $A$ is a real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ must have all real eigenvalues\n2. $A$ is diagonalizable\n3. If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are two eigenvectors, then $\\mathbf{u} \\perp \\mathbf{v}$"] [17592186161811 :question/worked-solution "Given that $A$ is an $n \\times n$ symmetric matrix over $\\mathbb{R}$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the eigenvalues of a symmetric matrix are real.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable. Hence, it is diagonalizable.\n- Statement III is false. According to the theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to *different* eigenvalues must be orthogonal, but two eigenvectors corresponding to the *same* eigenvalue may not be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186161812 :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $\\mathbf x\\in \\mathbb R^2$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1$ of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186161812 :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\lambda_1 \\mathbf u_1\\cdot \\mathbf u_1^T + \\lambda_2 \\mathbf u_2\\cdot \\mathbf u_2^T\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_1$ and $\\mathbf u_2$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$, respectively.\n\nMoreover, $\\mathbf u_i\\cdot \\mathbf u_i^T$ is the projection matrix corresponding to $\\mathbf u_i$.\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda=-1$. Computing $A-(-1)I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - (-1)I &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} - (-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + \\frac{1}{2}R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1=\\dfrac12x_2\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $x_2=2$, we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = \\sqrt{5}\n$$\n\nDividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu \\cdot u^{T} &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186161813 :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2 \\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=6$ and $\\lambda_2=-7$, respectively. Find the matrix $A$."] [17592186161813 :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\lambda_1 \\mathbf u_1\\cdot \\mathbf u_1^T + \\lambda_2 \\mathbf u_2\\cdot \\mathbf u_2^T\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_1$ and $\\mathbf u_2$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$, respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_{1} &= \\frac{v_{1}}{‖v_{1}‖} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nu_{2} &= \\frac{v_{2}}{‖v_{2}‖} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= λ_{1}u_{1}u_{1}^{T} + λ_{2}u_{2}u_{2}^{T} \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} + (-7) \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} + (-7) \\cdot \\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{13}(6 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} + (-7) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{13}(6 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix} + (-7) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}9 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-39 & 78 \\\\ 78 & 26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186162653 :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2 \\cdot 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues $λ_{1} = 9$ and $λ_{2} =-8$, respectively. Find the matrix $A$."] [17592186162653 :question/answer-fields 17592186161180] [17592186162671 :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2 \\cdot 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues $λ_{1} = 5$ and $λ_{2} =-5$, respectively. Find the matrix $A$."] [17592186162671 :question/answer-fields 17592186161288] [17592186228930 :question/problem "Consider a spherical planet with radius $(6.3\\times10^6)\\,\\textrm{m}$ and surface gravitational acceleration $g = 9.7\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$. Which differential equation best models the velocity $v$ of an object projected radially outward from the planet, where $r$ is the distance from the planet's center to the object? Assume the only force acting on the object is the force due to gravity."] [17592186228930 :question/worked-solution "According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the magnitude of the inward gravitational force pulling the object toward the planet is\n\n$$\nF_\\text{in} = G\\,\\dfrac{mM}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere $G$ is the gravitational constant, $m$ is the object's mass, and $M$ is the planet's mass.\n\nSince this is the only force and it acts inward, the resultant force, taking *outward* as positive, is\n\n$$\nF=-F_\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma$, where $a$ is the acceleration of the object, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= -F_{in} \\\\\nma &= -G\\frac{mM}{r^{2}} \\\\\na &= -\\frac{GM}{r^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the distance,\n\n$$\na=-\\dfrac{k}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwith constant of proportionality $k=GM > 0$.\n\nWhen at the surface,\n\n$$\nr=(6.3\\times10^6)\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nthe acceleration of the object due to gravity is\n\n$$\na=-9.7\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting into our previous equation, we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-9.7 &= - (k)/((6.3 \\cdot 10^{6})^{2}) \\\\\nk &= 9.7 \\cdot (6.3 \\cdot 10^{6})^{2} \\\\\n&= 9.7 \\cdot (6.3)^{2} \\cdot (10^{6})^{2} \\\\\n&= 384.993 \\cdot 10^{12} \\\\\n&= 3.84993 \\cdot 10^{14}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the equation, we get\n\n$$\na = -\\dfrac{3.84993\\times10^{14}}{r^2}\n$$\n\nFinally, since acceleration $a$ is the derivative of velocity $v$, which is the derivative of distance $r$, we can express acceleration as follows using the chain rule, treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{dr}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{dv}{dr} \\\\\n&= v\\frac{dv}{dr}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable differential equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\nv\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}r} = -\\dfrac{3.84993\\times10^{14}}{r^2}\n$$"] [17592186228931 :question/problem "Consider a spherical planet with radius $(8\\times10^5)\\,\\textrm{m}$ and surface gravitational acceleration $g = 16\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$. Which equation best models the velocity $v$ of an object projected radially outward from the planet's surface with an initial velocity of $(9\\times10^{4})\\,\\textrm{m/s}$, where $r$ is the distance from the planet's center to the object. Assume the only force acting on the object is the force due to gravity."] [17592186228931 :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the planet's radius and $g$ be the planet's surface gravitational acceleration.\n\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the magnitude of the inward gravitational force pulling the object toward the planet is\n\n$$\nF_\\text{in} = G\\,\\dfrac{mM}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere $G$ is the gravitational constant, $m$ is the object's mass, and $M$ is the planet's mass.\n\nSince this is the only force and it acts inward, the resultant force, taking *outward* as positive, is\n\n$$\nF=-F_\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma$, where $a$ is the acceleration of the object, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= -F_{in} \\\\\nma &= -G\\frac{mM}{r^{2}} \\\\\na &= -\\frac{GM}{r^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the distance,\n\n$$\na=-\\dfrac{k}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwith constant of proportionality $k=GM > 0$.\n\nWhen at the surface, $r=R$, the acceleration of the object due to gravity is $a=-g$. Substituting into our previous equation, we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n-g = -\\dfrac{k}{R^2} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k = gR^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the equation, we get\n\n$$\na = -\\dfrac{gR^2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nNow, since acceleration $a$ is the derivative of velocity $v$, which is the derivative of distance $r$, we can express acceleration as follows using the chain rule, treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{dr}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{dv}{dr} \\\\\n&= v\\frac{dv}{dr}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable differential equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\nv\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}r} = -\\dfrac{gR^2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nSolving using separation of variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\intv\\frac{dv}{dr}dr &= \\int(-\\frac{gR^{2}}{r^{2}})dr \\\\\n\\intvdv &= -gR^{2}\\int\\frac{1}{r^{2}}dr \\\\\n\\frac{v^{2}}{2} &= -gR^{2}(-\\frac{1}{r}) + C_{1} \\\\\nv^{2} &= \\frac{2gR^{2}}{r} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C=2C_1$ is a constant of integration.\n\nLet $v_0$ be the initial velocity of the object. Applying the initial condition $v(R) = v_0$, we can solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(R)^{2} &= \\frac{2gR^{2}}{R} + C \\\\\nv_{0}^{2} &= 2gR + C \\\\\nC &= v_{0}^{2} - 2gR\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for the velocity of the object is\n\n$$\nv^2 = \\dfrac{2gR^2}{r} + v_0^2 - 2gR\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting in the values\n\n$$\nR=(8\\times10^5)\n$$\n\n$g=16$, and\n\n$$\nv_0=(9\\times10^{4})\n$$\n\nwe conclude that the equation for the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv^{2} &= (2 \\cdot 16 \\cdot (8 \\cdot 10^{5})^{2})/(r) + (9 \\cdot 10^{4})^{2} - 2 \\cdot 16 \\cdot (8 \\cdot 10^{5}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2048 \\cdot 10^{10}}{r} + (81 \\cdot 10^{8}) - (256 \\cdot 10^{5}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2.048 \\cdot 10^{13}}{r} + (8.1 \\cdot 10^{9}) - (0.0256 \\cdot 10^{9}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2.048 \\cdot 10^{13}}{r} + ((8.1 - 0.0256) \\cdot 10^{9}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2.048 \\cdot 10^{13}}{r} + (8.0744 \\cdot 10^{9})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186228932 :question/problem "Consider a planet with radius $(1.2\\times10^{8})\\,\\textrm{m}$ and surface gravitational acceleration $g = 18.9\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$.\nWhat is the escape velocity of an object launched radially outward from the planet's surface? Assume only the force due to gravity acts on the object,\n\nExpress your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate."] [17592186228932 :question/worked-solution "Escape velocity is the minimum launch speed from a planet’s surface required to escape its gravity and reach infinity, neglecting all forces except gravity.\n\nLet $R$ be the planet's radius and $g$ be the planet's surface gravitational acceleration.\n\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the magnitude of the inward gravitational force pulling the object toward the planet is\n\n$$\nF_\\text{in} = G\\,\\dfrac{mM}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere $G$ is the gravitational constant, $m$ is the object's mass, $M$ is the planet's mass, and $r$ is the distance from the planet's center to the object.\n\nSince this is the only force and it acts inward, the resultant force, taking *outward* as positive, is\n\n$$\nF=-F_\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma$, where $a$ is the acceleration of the object, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= -F_{in} \\\\\nma &= -G\\frac{mM}{r^{2}} \\\\\na &= -\\frac{GM}{r^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the distance,\n\n$$\na=-\\dfrac{k}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwith constant of proportionality $k=GM > 0$.\n\nWhen at the surface, $r=R$, the acceleration of the object due to gravity is $a=-g$. Substituting into our previous equation, we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n-g = -\\dfrac{k}{R^2} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k = gR^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the equation, we get\n\n$$\na = -\\dfrac{gR^2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nNow, since acceleration $a$ is the derivative of velocity $v$, which is the derivative of distance $r$, we can express acceleration as follows using the chain rule, treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{dr}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{dv}{dr} \\\\\n&= v\\frac{dv}{dr}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable differential equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\nv\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}r} = -\\dfrac{gR^2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nSolving using separation of variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\intv\\frac{dv}{dr}dr &= \\int(-\\frac{gR^{2}}{r^{2}})dr \\\\\n\\intvdv &= -gR^{2}\\int\\frac{1}{r^{2}}dr \\\\\n\\frac{v^{2}}{2} &= -gR^{2}(-\\frac{1}{r}) + C_{1} \\\\\nv^{2} &= \\frac{2gR^{2}}{r} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C=2C_1$ is a constant of integration.\n\nLet $v_0$ be the initial velocity of the object. Applying the initial condition $v(R) = v_0$, we can solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(R)^{2} &= \\frac{2gR^{2}}{R} + C \\\\\nv_{0}^{2} &= 2gR + C \\\\\nC &= v_{0}^{2} - 2gR\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for the velocity of the object is\n\n$$\nv^2 = \\dfrac{2gR^2}{r} + v_0^2 - 2gR\n$$\n\nFor the object to escape the planet's gravity, we require a positive initial velocity, and for the velocity to never equal zero.\n\nNotice in our velocity equation that as $r \\to \\infty$, the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2gR^2}{r}\n$$\n\napproaches zero. So, for the velocity to never vanish, we must have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}v_{0}^{2} - 2gR & \\ge 0 \\\\ v_{0}^{2} & \\ge 2gR \\\\ v_{0} & \\ge \\sqrt{2gR}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum velocity of the object to escape the surface of the planet is\n\n$$\nv_e = \\sqrt{2gR}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting in the values\n\n$$\nR=(1.2\\times10^{8})\n$$\n\nand $g=18.9$, we conclude that the escape velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv_{e} &= \\sqrt{2 \\cdot (1.2 \\cdot 10^{8}) \\cdot 18.9} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(2 \\cdot 1.2 \\cdot 18.9) \\cdot 10^{8}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{45.36} \\cdot \\sqrt{10^{8}} \\\\\n&\\approx (6.73 \\cdot 10^{4})m/s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three significant figures."] [17592186167191 :question/problem "$x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t) + \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(f(t)), Φ = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{2t} & -3e^{4t} \\\\ 0 & 2e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute $Φ^{-1}f(t)$ and the particular solution $x_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n| $Φ^{-1}f(t) =$ |  | $-10e^{-2t}$ |  | $$, | $x_{p}(t) =$ |  | $2$ |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |  |  |  |"] [17592186167191 :question/answer-fields 17592186164141] [17592186167191 :question/answer-fields 17592186164142] [17592186125137 :question/problem "Consider the basis $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}, b_{3}}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $C$ of $R^{2}$ given by\n$C = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}$.\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B \\mid C}$ of the map $T: V → R^{2}$ relative to these bases if\n$T(b_{1}) = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, T(b_{2}) = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, T(b_{3}) = \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186125137 :question/answer-fields 17592186123392] [17592186125111 :question/problem "Find a basis of $Ker(T)$ given that $T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-9x - 3y \\\\ -12x - 4y\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186125111 :question/answer-fields 17592186123236] [17592186171840 :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand the curve $\\mathcal C$, defined as\n$\\mathcal C:\\: 2x_1^2+6x_1x_2+ 2x_2^2 -2x_1+2x_2 =7$.\n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(\\mathbf{x})=2x_1^2+6x_1x_2+2x_2^2$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $5y_1^2-y_2^2$, what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $\\mathcal C$?"] [17592186171840 :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\: \\underbrace{2x_1^2+6x_1x_2+2x_2^2}_{Q(\\mathbf{x})} -2x_1+2x_2 =7\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(\\mathbf x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $\\mathcal C$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x_{1}^{2} + 6x_{1}x_{2} + 2x_{2}^{2} - 2x_{1} + 2x_{2} &= 7 \\\\\n5y_{1}^{2} - y_{2}^{2} - 2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}y_{1} - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}y_{2}) + 2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}y_{1} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}y_{2}) &= 7 \\\\\n5y_{1}^{2} - y_{2}^{2} + \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}y_{2} &= 7 \\\\\n5y_{1}^{2} - y_{2}^{2} + 2\\sqrt{2}y_{2} &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable $y_2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5y_{1}^{2} - y_{2}^{2} + 2\\sqrt{2}y_{2} &= 7 \\\\\n5y_{1}^{2} - (y_{2}^{2} - 2\\sqrt{2}y_{2}) &= 7 \\\\\n5y_{1}^{2} - (y_{2}^{2} - 2\\sqrt{2}y_{2} + (\\sqrt{2})^{2}) + 2 &= 7 \\\\\n5y_{1}^{2} - (y_{2}^{2} - 2\\sqrt{2}y_{2} + (\\sqrt{2})^{2}) + (\\sqrt{2})^{2} &= 7 \\\\\n5y_{1}^{2} - (y_{2} - \\sqrt{2})^{2} + 2 &= 7 \\\\\n5y_{1}^{2} - (y_{2} - \\sqrt{2})^{2} &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n{z_{1} = y_{1},; z_{2} = y_{2} - \\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5y_{1}^{2} - (y_{2} - \\sqrt{2})^{2} &= 5 \\\\\n5z_{1}^{2} - z_{2}^{2} &= 5 \\\\\n\\frac{z_{1}^{2}}{1^{2}} - (z_{2}^{2})/((\\sqrt{5})^{2}) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents a hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $\\sqrt5$."] [17592186171841 :question/problem "Which of the following equations define a non-empty set of points in the $x_1x_2$-plane?\n\n1. $x_1^2 +y_2^2= 0$\n2. $x_1^2+\\dfrac{x_2^2}{2} = -1$\n3. $2x_2^2 -x_1^2= 0$"] [17592186171841 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the equations in turn.\n\n- The equation $x_1^2 +y_2^2= 0$ defines a non-empty set. It represents the point $(0,0)$ in the $x_1x_2$-plane.\n- The equation $x_1^2+\\dfrac{x_2^2}{2} = -1$ defines an empty set. Notice that the left-hand side is non-negative for all $x_1$ and $x_2$, while the right-hand side is negative.\n- The equation $2x_2^2 -x_1^2= 0$ defines a non-empty set. It represents a pair of intersecting lines, $x_1 =\\sqrt{2}x_2$ and $x_1 =-\\sqrt{2}x_2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186171842 :question/problem "What type of a quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n$3x_1^2 + 2x_1 - x_2 =\\dfrac{5}{3}$"] [17592186171842 :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the squares for the variables $x_1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x_{1}^{2} + 2x_{1} - x_{2} &= \\frac{5}{3} \\\\\n3(x_{1}^{2} + \\frac{2}{3}x_{1}) - x_{2} &= \\frac{5}{3} \\\\\n3(x_{1}^{2} + \\frac{2}{3}x_{1} + (\\frac{1}{3})^{2} - (\\frac{1}{3})^{2}) - x_{2} &= \\frac{5}{3} \\\\\n3(x_{1} + \\frac{1}{3})^{2} - 3(\\frac{1}{3})^{2} - x_{2} &= \\frac{5}{3} \\\\\n3(x_{1} + \\frac{1}{3})^{2} - \\frac{1}{3} - x_{2} &= \\frac{5}{3} \\\\\n3(x_{1} + \\frac{1}{3})^{2} &= x_{2} + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n{y_{1} = x_{1} + \\frac{1}{3},; y_{2} = x_{2} + 2\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3y_{1}^{2} &= y_{2} \\\\\ny_{1}^{2} &= \\frac{1}{3}y_{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive $y_2$-axis."] [17592186172631 :question/problem "Which of the following equations define a non-empty set of points in the $x_{1}x_{2}$-plane?\n\n1. $x_{1}^{2} = 6$\n2. $x_{1}^{2} + \\frac{x_{2}^{2}}{5} =-1$\n3. $x_{2}^{2} =-7$"] [17592186172631 :question/answer-fields 17592186171381] [17592186176418 :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $m/s^{2}$, given by the function $a(t) = 2t - 4$, where $t > 0$ is the time in seconds. The positive direction is left to right. If the particle has velocity $v = 12m/s$ when $t = 6s$, determine the time intervals in which the particle moves to the left."] [17592186176418 :question/answer-fields 17592186174726] [17592186176510 :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $m/s^{2}$, given by the function $a(t) = 3 - 3t^{2}$, where $t⩾0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v = 2m/s$ when $t = 2s$, calculate the maximum velocity $v_{max}$ of the particle and determine the time whan the particle reaches its maximum velocity."] [17592186176510 :question/answer-fields 17592186175168] [17592186083086 :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola \n![](images/57/57e159560b79e8383e6a2eec7a8f8dd6568fbf0fb8dae9f5277d35a166b6402a.png)"] [17592186083086 :question/worked-solution "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at ![](images/6c/6c305528045f15b88bbb08bcbb70e321036d4c901b971d2d9f37ec0fc14c5bab.png) is given by\n![](images/42/42df0f3278c4cf754f5e3c65787c2640a0c084ab1e79874dd3c071a97012b0dd.png)\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group ![](images/6c/6c64f050494f28920cf5518293243a78d46e5fa2758fc8f470fdb466586effbc.png) and ![](images/75/751725803c8443e8ccea32f24624965f57b739dab31251b837c5a86ecee96f43.png) terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n![](images/fc/fc13c27b687a2bfbf369fc03321ee89736bd1b2383871bf8ceac7457e2c35591.png)\n\nTherefore, the center is ![](images/dd/dd3932b5cb055c4ae37e61384441deddc8626e4257ceb60b596325981b64710d.png)"] [17592186083569 :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola\n$-2x^{2} + 5y^{2} + 12x - 60y =-152$?"] [17592186083569 :question/answer-fields 17592186080279] [17592186152625 :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$t^{2}y^{″} - 9ty^{′} + 41y = 0, y(1) = 1, y^{′}(1) = 1, t > 0$."] [17592186152625 :question/answer-fields 17592186150227] [17592186125149 :question/problem "The basis $B$ of $R^{3}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T: R^{3} → R^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$B = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}, [T]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}a & 1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & b & -5 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} & c\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhat is the value of $a + b + c$, given that\n\n$T(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}) = \\begin{bmatrix}x + y + z \\\\ x - y \\\\ x - z\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186125149 :question/answer-fields 17592186123464] [17592186124824 :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x, y, z) =-x^{2} - y - z^{2}$ on the surface $y^{2} - x^{2} = 1$.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global maximum of $f(x, y, z)$ exists on the surface.*"] [17592186124824 :question/answer-fields 17592186121511] [17592186120275 :question/problem "What is the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t) = ⟨t + 1, 8\\ln t, 2t^{2} + 3⟩$ at the point $P(2, 0, 5)$?"] [17592186120275 :question/answer-fields 17592186117759] [17592186097268 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b$, given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^{b}f(x)dx = lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 0)^(n - 1)[(2 + \\frac{5k}{n})^{2} \\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]$."] [17592186097268 :question/answer-fields 17592186095502] [17592186161822 :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the sum or difference between two symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n![](images/f8/f8d153708f7872d0c96414a77c12379d40c6b60a5f08844945fb8a8f8c1809ab.png)\nTheir product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186162700 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. If $A$ is *any* $n \\cdot n$ matrix, then $A - A^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n2. The sum of two $n \\cdot n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n3. If $A$ is symmetric, then $A^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)."] [17592186162700 :question/answer-fields 17592186161462] [17592186175725 :question/problem "Consider the standard matrices of three linear transformations shown below. Which of these transformations map $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto a line?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186175725 :question/worked-solution "Given a linear transformation over $\\mathbb{R}^2$ with the standard matrix $T$, we have the following:\n\n- $T$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto $\\mathbb{R}^2$ if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=2$, i.e., $T$ is nonsingular.\n- $T$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto a line if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=1$.\n- $T$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto $(0,0)$ if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=0$, i.e., $T$ is the zero matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $T_1$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto a line. Notice that the columns of $T_1$ are linearly dependent but it's not the zero matrix. So, $\\text{rank}(T_1)=1$.\n- $T_2$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto $\\mathbb{R}^2$. Indeed, $T_2$ is a nonsingular matrix, i.e. $\\text{rank}(T_2)=2$.\n- $T_3$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto $(0,0)$ since $T_3$ is the zero matrix, i.e. $\\text{rank}(T_3)=0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$T_1$ only.\""] [17592186175726 :question/problem "Consider the standard matrix $T$ of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the equation of the line that the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto."] [17592186175726 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, yet $T$ is not the zero matrix.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=1\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf{T}$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the points $(6,2)$ and $(-9,-3)$, which are defined by the columns of $T$.\n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{-3-2}{-9-6} = \\dfrac13\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{T}$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto the line\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac13x\n$$"] [17592186175727 :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto the line $y=-5x$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\langle -1, 5 \\rangle \\in \\text{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$\n2. $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T})=\\{0\\}$\n3. $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\Bbb R^2$"] [17592186175727 :question/worked-solution "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ with the standard matrix $T$ is a one-dimensional line, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(T) = \\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = 1\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nnullity(T) = nullity(T) &= 2 - rank(T) \\\\\n&= 2 - 1 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the point $(-1,5)$ satisfies the equation of the line $y=-5x$ since $5=-5\\cdot (-1)$. Hence,\n$\\langle -1, 5 \\rangle \\in \\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$.\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\textrm{nullity}(T)=1$, we get\n$\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\{\\mathbf0\\}$.\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=1$, we get\n$\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\Bbb R^2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186176275 :question/problem "Consider the standard matrices of three linear transformations shown below. Which of the transformations map $R^{2}$ onto $(0, 0)$?\n\n$T_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, T_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}, T_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186176275 :question/answer-fields 17592186173878] [17592186176482 :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps $R^{2}$ to the origin. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $rank(T) = 1$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$\n2. $Im(T)$ contains no vectors of $R^{2}$\n3. $Ker(T) = R^{2}$"] [17592186176482 :question/answer-fields 17592186175000] [17592186180104 :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the positive $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $xm$, the velocity is $vm/s$, and the acceleration is $k\\sqrt{x}m/s^{2}$, where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v = 6m/s$ at the origin and the particle has velocity $v = 6\\sqrt{6}m/s$ when its displacement is $9m$, find the value of $k$."] [17592186180104 :question/answer-fields 17592186178263] [17592186176485 :question/problem "Let $v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}a_{1} \\\\ b_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}a_{2} \\\\ b_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nGiven that $λ_{1} = 2\\textrm{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A$, find the value of $\\frac{a_{1}a_{2}}{b_{1}b_{2}}$."] [17592186176485 :question/answer-fields 17592186175018] [17592186115066 :question/problem "Consider the matrix\n![](images/a5/a58c9caa0812b56d74f38a457c3f39e72a3fd365a881d4128cfd55c1773e05f6.png)\nFind $f(D)$ if $f(X)=-X^2+3X+2.$"] [17592186179557 :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x \\geq 1$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,\\rm{m/s}$, the magnitude of its acceleration is $(2x-2) \\, \\rm{m/s}^2$, and the acceleration is directed toward $O$.\nIf $P$ has velocity $\\sqrt2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ when its displacement is $1\\,\\rm{m}$, find the value of $x$ for which $P$ is instantaneously at rest."] [17592186179557 :question/worked-solution "![](images/0c/0c47a1e988a75e4813c92a76acbf3a821364995123f3eb394b07d4bfaa95f80d.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $(2x-2)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$ and is directed *toward* $O$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = -(2x-2)\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = -(2x-2)\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} \\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) \\textrm{d}x = \\int (-2x+2) \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12v^2 = -x^2+2x + C\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{2}\\,\\rm{m/s}\n$$\n\nwhen\n\n$$\nx=1\\,\\rm{m}\n$$\n\nwe can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}v^{2} &= -x^{2} + 2x + C \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{2})^{2} &= - (1)^{2} + 2(1) + C \\\\\nC &= 1 + 1 - 2 \\\\\nC &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12v^2 = -x^2+2x\n$$\n\nFinally, the particle is instantaneously at rest when\n\n$$\nv(x) = 0\\,\\rm{m/s}\n$$\n\nWe find the displacement of the particle when instantaneously at rest by substituting $v=0$ in the equation above and solving for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}v^{2} &= -x^{2} + 2x \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} &= -x^{2} + 2x \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution of this equation for\n\n$$\nx \\geq 1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nx=2\\,\\rm{m}\n$$"] [17592186179558 :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,\\rm{m}$, the velocity is $v\\,\\rm{m/s}$, and the acceleration is $2kx\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$, where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ at the origin and the particle has velocity $v=4\\,\\rm{m/s}$ when its displacement is $1 \\,\\rm{m}$, find the value of $k$."] [17592186179558 :question/worked-solution "![](images/62/6280c4b1561cda0d16c6758bb5d8fae30d66d9925bd1bb9db1a29e70093c6f61.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $2kx\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} =2 kx\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = 2kx\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) \\textrm{d}x = \\int 2kx \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12v^2 = kx^2 + C\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nv=2\\,\\rm{m/s}\n$$\n\nwhen\n\n$$\nx=0\\,\\rm{m}\n$$\n\nwe can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}v^{2} &= kx^{2} + C \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} &= k(0)^{2} + C \\\\\nC &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12v^2 = kx^2 + 2\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\nv=4\\,\\rm{m/s}\n$$\n\nwhen\n\n$$\nx=1\\,\\rm{m}\n$$\n\nwe can find the value of the positive constant $k$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}v^{2} &= kx^{2} + 2 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} &= k(1)^{2} + 2 \\\\\n8 &= k + 2 \\\\\nk &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179583 :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x \\geq 0$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x \\, \\rm{m}$, the velocity is $v \\, \\rm{m/s}$, and the acceleration is $k(5 - x) \\, \\rm{m/s}^2$, where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v = \\sqrt{6} \\, \\rm{m/s}$ at the origin and has velocity $v = 2\\sqrt{6} \\, \\rm{m/s}$ when its displacement is $1 \\, \\rm{m}$, find the maximum velocity of the particle."] [17592186179583 :question/worked-solution "![](images/c1/c129b3157bf3741f4e1fa30c7bb97eaa0bff11ff1eef1e2ee548f2653a4da7d2.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v$. Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k(5 - x)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = k(5 - x)\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = 0\n$$\n\nand test each solution using the first derivative test.\n\nSolving\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = 0\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk(5 - x) &= 0 \\\\\n5 - x &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when\n\n$$\nx = 5 \\, \\rm{m}\n$$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,5)$ | $5$ | $(5,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Test Point | $4$ |  | $6$ |\n| $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}$ | $k > 0$ | $0$ | $-k < 0$ |\n| $v$ | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ | max | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when\n\n$$\nx = 5\\,\\rm{m}\n$$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = k(5 - x)\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) \\textrm{d}x = \\int k(5 - x) \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2 = k\\left(5x - \\dfrac{1}{2}x^2 \\right) + C\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nv = \\sqrt{6} \\, \\rm{m/s}\n$$\n\nwhen\n\n$$\nx = 0 \\, \\rm{m}\n$$\n\nwe can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}v^{2} &= k(5x - \\frac{1}{2}x^{2}) + C \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{6})^{2} &= k(5(0) - \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}) + C \\\\\nC &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2 = k\\left(5x - \\dfrac{1}{2}x^2 \\right) + 3\n$$\n\nAlso, since\n\n$$\nv = 2\\sqrt{6} \\, \\rm{m/s}\n$$\n\nwhen\n\n$$\nx = 1 \\, \\rm{m}\n$$\n\nwe can find the value of the positive constant $k$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}v^{2} &= k(5x - \\frac{1}{2}x^{2}) + 3 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{6})^{2} &= k(5(1) - \\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}) + 3 \\\\\n12 &= \\frac{9k}{2} + 3 \\\\\n\\frac{9k}{2} &= 9 \\\\\nk &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}v^{2} &= 2(5x - \\frac{1}{2}x^{2}) + 3 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}v^{2} &= 10x - x^{2} + 3 \\\\\nv^{2} &= 20x - 2x^{2} + 6 \\\\\nv &= ± \\sqrt{-2x^{2} + 20x + 6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x$, we have that $v(x) \\gt 0$, so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x) = \\sqrt{-2x^2 + 20x + 6}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\nv_{\\max} = v(5) =\\sqrt{-2(5)^2 + 20(5) + 6} = 2\\sqrt{14}\\,\\rm{m/s}\n$$"] [17592186179919 :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x$ meters, its velocity is $vm/s$, the magnitude of its acceleration is $2xm/s^{2}$, and the acceleration is directed toward $O$.\nIf $P$ has velocity $4m/s$ when its displacement is $1m$, find the values of $x$ for which $P$ is instantaneously at rest."] [17592186179919 :question/answer-fields 17592186177153] [17592186179920 :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the positive $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $xm$, the velocity is $vm/s$, the magnitude of the acceleration is $kx^{2}m/s^{2}$, where $k$ is a positive constant, and the acceleration is directed toward $O$. If $P$ has velocity $v = 16m/s$ at the origin and the particle is at rest when its displacement is $20m$, find the value of $k$."] [17592186179920 :question/answer-fields 17592186177159] [17592186180102 :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x \\ge 0$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $xm$, the velocity is $vm/s$, and the acceleration is $(3 - x)m/s^{2}$. If $P$ has velocity $v = 2m/s$ at the origin, find the maximum velocity of the particle."] [17592186180102 :question/answer-fields 17592186178251] [17592186097052 :question/problem "![](images/c2/c2406e6f3cb76f50bfcc73a348b38d93b7a24da8e4314e0b3523bb4cbf143364.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $OA^(→) = a$, $OB^(→) = b$, and $T$ divides $AB^(―)$ in the ratio $AT: TB = 1: 1$. Find $OT^(→)$."] [17592186097052 :question/answer-fields 17592186094255] [17592186231297 :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n$y'-6y = f(x), \\qquad y(1)=4$\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $2$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution $y_p$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution $y_p$ satisfies $y_p(1)=0$.*\n\n$AAA?AAA$"] [17592186231297 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = f(x)\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\ny = y_c + y_p\n$$\n\nwhere $y_c$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and $y_p$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of $y' - 6y = 0$ is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator $y' - 6y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x) \\sim Ae^{6x} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (a_n \\cos(n\\pi x) + b_n \\sin(n\\pi x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(1)=4$. Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(1) &= Ae^{6(1)} + y_{p}(1) \\\\\n4 &= Ae^{6} + 0 \\\\\nA &= 4e^{-6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\ny_c = (4e^{-6})e^{6x} = 4e^{6x-6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x) \\sim \\bbox[4pt,border: 0.5px solid black]{4e^{6x-6}} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (a_n \\cos(n\\pi x) + b_n \\sin(n\\pi x))\n$$"] [17592186231299 :question/problem "$y''+ 7\\pi^2 y = f(x), \\qquad y(0)=0, \\quad y'(0)=0$\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an odd, $4$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$AAA?AAA$"] [17592186231299 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = f(x)\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\ny = y_c + y_p\n$$\n\nwhere $y_c$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and $y_p$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution $y_p$. Since $f(x)$ is an odd function, its Fourier series consists only of sine terms. For a second-order equation with no $y'$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{16(-1)^n}{n^2\\pi^3\\left(28-n^2\\right)} \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + 7\\pi^2 = 0\n$$\n\ngives roots\n\n$$\n\\lambda = \\pm \\sqrt{7}\\pi \\textrm i\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny_c = A\\cos(\\sqrt{7}\\pi x) + B\\sin(\\sqrt{7}\\pi x)\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Using $y(0)=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(0) &= A\\cos (0) + B\\sin (0) + y_{p}(0) \\\\\n0 &= A(1) + 0 + 0 \\\\\nA &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $y_p(0)=0$ because the particular solution is a sine series.\n\nNext, we use the derivative condition $y'(0)=0$. First, we compute the derivative of the total solution (with $A=0$):\n\n$$\ny'(x) \\sim B(\\sqrt{7}\\pi)\\cos(\\sqrt{7}\\pi x) + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{8(-1)^n}{n\\pi^2\\left(28-n^2\\right)} \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nApplying $y'(0)=0$ and noting that $\\cos(0)=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 &= B(\\sqrt{7}π) + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(8(-1)^{n})/(nπ^{2}(28 - n^{2})) \\\\\nB(\\sqrt{7}π) &= - ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(8(-1)^{n})/(nπ^{2}(28 - n^{2})) \\\\\nB &= -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}π}∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(8(-1)^{n})/(nπ^{2}(28 - n^{2})) \\\\\n&= - ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(8(-1)^{n})/(\\sqrt{7}nπ^{3}(28 - n^{2}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $B$ back into\n\n$$\ny_c = B\\sin(\\sqrt{7}\\pi x)\n$$\n\nwe find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)\\sim \\bbox[4pt,border: 0.5px solid black]{-\\sin(\\sqrt{7}\\pi x)\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{8(-1)^n}{\\sqrt{7}n\\pi^3\\left(28-n^2\\right)}} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{16(-1)^n}{n^2\\pi^3\\left(28-n^2\\right)} \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$"] [17592186129881 :question/problem "The domain of the function $f(x) = \\frac{x + 3}{3x - 2a}$ is $(- ∞, 2)∪(2, ∞)$. What is the value of $a$?"] [17592186129881 :question/answer-fields 17592186128737] [17592186188210 :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3 \\to \\mathbb{R}^3$. If $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=1$, then what is $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})$?"] [17592186188210 :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) + \\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = n\n$$\n\nFor the given transformation, we have $n=3$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nrank(T) + nullity(T) &= n \\\\\nrank(T) + 1 &= 3 \\\\\nrank(T) &= 3 - 1 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186188213 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbb{R}^4 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^4$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T$, and let $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^4$. Consider the following statement:\n\n> *If $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$, then $\\textrm{__________}$.*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n1. $\\textrm{rank}(T)=3$\n2. $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=3$\n3. $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection"] [17592186188213 :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^4$ into $\\mathbb{R}^4$, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n> $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $Im(T) = R^{4}; rank(T) = 4; Ker(T) = {0}; nullity(T) = 0$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) + \\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = 4\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ and the set $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\textrm{rank}(T)=\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=3$.\n- Statement II is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n$nullity(T)|= 4 - rank(T); = 4 - 3; = 1$.\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq 4$ (or since $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq 0$), the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"I only.\""] [17592186188222 :question/problem "Find $\\text{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})$ given that $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3\\to\\mathbb{R}^3$ and $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\{[x, y, z]^T \\,: \\, x=y=z\\}$."] [17592186188222 :question/worked-solution "Any vector from $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ x \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix} = x\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(v_{1})\n$$\n\nThis means that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=1\n$$\n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nnullity(T) &= 3 - rank(T) \\\\\n&= 3 - 1 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186188917 :question/problem "Let $T: R^{5} → R^{5}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T$, and let ${v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in $R^{5}$. Consider the following statement:\n\n> *If $Im(T) = Span{v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}}$, then $__________$.*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n1. $rank(T) = 1$\n2. $nullity(T) = 2$\n3. $T$ is a bijection"] [17592186188917 :question/answer-fields 17592186187548] [17592186188923 :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $T: R^{5} → R^{5}$. If $nullity(T) = 3$ then $rank(T) =$"] [17592186188923 :question/answer-fields 17592186187584] [17592186188930 :question/problem "Find $nullity(T)$ given that $T: R^{3} → R^{3}$ and\n$Im(T) = {[x, y, z]^{T}: - x + z = 0}$."] [17592186188930 :question/answer-fields 17592186187626] [17592186180010 :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$x = 3u - v + 2, y = v - u$.\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $det(A) =-2$\n3. The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving"] [17592186180010 :question/answer-fields 17592186177699] [17592186217806 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$y^{″} - 3y^{′} + 5y = 0$.\nSuppose $y_{1}$ and $y_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(1) = 2$. What is $W(4)$?$W(4) =$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186217806 :question/answer-fields 17592186216673] [17592186217809 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$y^{″} + \\frac{2}{x}y^{′} + y = 0, x > 0$.\nSuppose $y_{1}$ and $y_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(1) = 6$. What is $W(3)$?$W(3) =$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186217809 :question/answer-fields 17592186216684] [17592186175861 :question/problem "The random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $-0.5$ | $-0.25$ | $0$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.25$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ | $0.3$ |\n\nLet $Y=2-4X$. Calculate $P(Y \\gt 2)$."] [17592186175861 :question/worked-solution "First, let's add a $Y = 2-4X$ row to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $-0.5$ | $-0.25$ | $0$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $4$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.25$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ | $0.3$ |\n\nNow,\n\n$$\nf_X(x) = f_Y(y)\n$$\n\nfor corresponding values of $X$ and $Y$. So, the distribution of $Y$ is as follows:\n\n| $y$ | $4$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f_Y(y)$ | $0.25$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ | $0.3$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Y > 2) &= P(Y = 3) + P(Y = 4) \\\\\n&= 0.2 + 0.25 \\\\\n&= 0.45\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186175862 :question/problem "The random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $-0.5$ | $-0.25$ | $0$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.25$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ | $0.3$ |\n\nLet $Y=2-4X$. The distribution of $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $y$ | $a$ | $3$ | $c$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f_Y(y)$ | $0.25$ | $b$ | $0.1$ | $d$ | $0.3$ |\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c$?"] [17592186175862 :question/worked-solution "First, let's add a $Y = 2-4X$ row to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $-0.5$ | $-0.25$ | $0$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $4$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.25$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ | $0.3$ |\n\nNow,\n\n$$\nf_X(x) = f_Y(y)\n$$\n\nfor corresponding values of $X$ and $Y$. Therefore, the distribution of $Y$ is as follows:\n\n| $y$ | $4$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f_Y(y)$ | $0.25$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ | $0.3$ |\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\na=4, b=0.2, c=2\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\na+b+c =6.2\n$$"] [17592186176271 :question/problem "The random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f_{X}(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |\n\nLet $Y = 2X + 1$. The distribution of $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $y$ | $-3$ | $a$ | $1$ | $b$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $c$ | $0.2$ | $d$ |\n\nWhat is the value of $a + b + c$?"] [17592186176271 :question/answer-fields 17592186173854]]